WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Between a note and its octave, pitch is continuous.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.440
Yet musical cultures keep choosing a few steps.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.290
Often five.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.490
Seven.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:13.765
Twelve.

00:00:14.365 --> 00:00:15.805
Why so few?

00:00:16.805 --> 00:00:20.205
Each note you add forms an interval with every note already there.

00:00:20.245 --> 00:00:22.480
More harmony to use. And more to learn.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.035
Every note also had to be built.

00:00:25.335 --> 00:00:28.710
As building got cheaper, the palette could grow.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.725
Learning doesn't get cheaper in the same way.

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:38.945
In our model, if learning costs rise faster than the harmonic gains, the system settles at a finite size.

00:00:38.985 --> 00:00:41.580
Even when notes cost nothing to build.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.320
The model doesn't say the limit is twelve.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.945
Only that there is one.

00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:54.755
Then we measured 623 compositions, from 1485 to 1963.

00:00:54.795 --> 00:00:59.610
MIDI stores every note as one of twelve pitch classes.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.992
So it can't show a scale growing.

00:01:02.032 --> 00:01:04.880
Only how composers used the twelve.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:09.290
Before 1900, pieces used more of them, century by century.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.635
After 1900, the count levelled off, at nearly all twelve.

00:01:13.985 --> 00:01:15.685
The palette was full.

00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:17.515
Notes spread more evenly.

00:01:17.555 --> 00:01:19.535
And dissonance kept rising.

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:22.935
The palette stopped growing.

00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:24.500
The music didn't.
